title waymark filenames + commit messages debrided from all history, re-signed, force-pushed
prompt Keaton: the dead marks are still in the recent git log history -- the commits themselves must be rewritten so the old waymarks are not referenced, not even saying they were debrided; and check filenames themselves too
think The first waymark debride cleaned the living tree and shed 76 logs, yet left the superseded marks in two places git log still shows: commit messages (subjects and bodies) and file paths. Keaton is right -- a message that says a mark was debrided to its successor still references the mark, which defeats the purpose; and the pre-rename paths (the witness files, the retired module dir, the exploration docs) live on in history
think Surveyed both: 130 commits carry a mark token in their message, 232 distinct file paths carry one across history (all under our own dirs -- tools, session-logs, active-designing, pond, mandi, counsel, brushstroke, and the retired module dir -- none under vendor/vere, so precise slug renames carry no false-positive risk), plus 5 dead-mark paths still in the current tree (one counsel file and four archived logs under the retired module name)
think Chose filter-branch over filter-repo: filter-repo processes all 42k reflog-held commits and would rewrite the safety tags, while filter-branch touches only the ref range I name and leaves tags and remotes alone -- the same tool that cleanly dropped the urbit lineage and purged the 76 logs earlier
think Built three composed filters: a path-rename index-filter (git ls-files-s piped through a slug-boundary sed, rebuilt via update-index -- the coined module name swapped globally, the five four-letter marks only at separator boundaries so English words and shas are safe), a message rewrite msg-filter (token-swap to the living marks for the ~124 rung-label commits, plus full REWORDS for the six breach-announcement commits so no message states what a mark was renamed from), and a commit-filter re-sign
think Before the rewrite, genericized the debride's own records in the working tree: the CAIRNS walk-back rows and the debride session log now name only the living marks and generic "superseded waymarks"; the sealed registry's four notes dropped their "renamed from" clauses and the registry was re-sealed (witness GREEN, 30 marks re-derived). Planted a walk-back cairn at nib 55e684485a first
obs Ran the full-branch rewrite (2911 commits): paths renamed, messages rewritten, every commit re-signed. One residual slipped through -- a bare lowercase log-slug the boundary sed missed -- caught by verification and fixed in a second message-only re-sign pass; the one English hyphenation that legitimately carries those letters was correctly left untouched
obs Verified GREEN before pushing: zero true mark tokens in any commit message (only the English hyphenation remains), zero mark tokens in any file path across all history, current tree paths and content clean, zero unsigned commits, registry witness GREEN, renamed witnesses (the living-mark witnesses) present with content preserved. Force-pushed origin + xykj61 (55e684485a...0ac0177ade)
obs Left as dated testimony, honestly flagged: the file CONTENT of older counsel prose and kept session logs still mentions the marks in passing (a broader content sweep would rewrite dated testimony and risks the corpus fixture, which holds several of the superseded four-letter marks as legitimate dictionary words) -- git log and filenames, Keaton's named targets, are fully clean
file crux/CAIRNS.md walk-back cairn for this rewrite (nib 55e684485a); older rows genericized
file crux/waymark-registry.bron four notes de-eldered and re-sealed; witness GREEN
file session-logs/20260817-232601_waymark-elder-deep-debride.kyri prior log genericized to living marks
recommend check-in the filename + message debride is landed and verified on both remotes -- git log and every file path are elder-free, every commit re-signed. A fresh clone reads clean; every clone must re-clone. Remaining marks live only in dated-testimony file content (counsel, kept logs), which I can sweep next if wanted, weighing the corpus-fixture guard
